# TODO - Performance regression contracts

## Objective

Measure useful throughput without degrading fidelity.

## Acceptance

- Measure frame times, FPS, latency, memory, loading and binary size.
- Use representative workloads and comparable quality settings.
- Keep GPU acceleration, CPU work and cache budgets workload-driven.

## Evidence

Partial evidence, 2026-11-24: fresh validation passed in run
1795575501611655948-2483905 (72 tests).

`games::performance::PerformanceCollector` aggregates frame durations, FPS,
input latency, resident-memory peaks, loading duration and binary size
from explicit host observations in constant memory. One bounded timing
summary provides sample counts, totals, maxima and nanosecond means for
both frame and input measurements. Missing input or memory measurements
remain absent rather than being fabricated as zero.

Synthetic tests verify units, measured zero versus absent data, maximum
representable durations, overflow atomicity (including partial frame
updates) and unchanged quality settings. The fixed-step clock computes
eligible ticks directly instead of scanning the backlog before updates.

No representative playable game, actual GPU timings or reproducible
platform benchmark matrix exists yet. Hardware-specific optimization
choices and acceptance measurements remain open.

The collector now records the explicit capped or uncapped presentation
policy in the quality profile. A caller may configure a separate nonzero
frame-time target; deadline misses are counted exactly in constant memory.
A frame at the target is not counted as missed, and rejected observations
cannot change the miss count. Tests demonstrate a 50 FPS mean with one
over-budget frame and confirm that a rendering cap alone does not imply a
benchmark deadline. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577195880786433-2782349 (91 workspace tests).

Controller detachment now uses the held-input map's controller-specific
key range rather than repeatedly scanning unrelated input sources. A test
covers both extreme controller identifiers, shared logical actions and
stable release order. Fresh validation passed in run
1795577450698187130-2832751 (95 workspace tests). No benchmark speedup is
claimed from this algorithmic change alone.

The native platform meter connects monotonic host completion timestamps
to the shared collector and rejects reversed intervals without changing
the previous timestamp. Load intervals and actual artifact byte sizes are
measured separately. Linux procfs resident-memory sampling is explicit
and reports invalid or unavailable data rather than a fabricated value.
Tests include the real test executable and Linux process resident size.
The native tests passed in fresh run 1795577633179381736-2865763.
Host completion age is not a physical display or GPU latency measurement.
Android procfs availability and actual game workloads remain unverified.

The Linux graphics adapter uses one bounded streaming RGBA texture for a
presentation session. The shared viewport determines the drawable fit,
and SDL uses nearest-neighbor sampling without requested vertical sync.
A display-independent software-canvas read-back verifies all pixels in
a scaled, letterboxed frame and a subsequent upload; a rejected short
frame leaves the previous pixels unchanged. An explicitly enabled hidden
native window also presented two frames and reported SDL's accelerated
renderer capability. Fresh validation with GAMES_NATIVE_RENDER_SMOKE=1
passed in run 1795582311633882091-3468901 (164 workspace tests).
The host emitted a nonfatal libdecor plugin initialization warning.
The capability flag does not prove physical GPU execution, measured
frame latency, scene throughput or title parity.

The native graphics adapter now also composes ordered sprite scenes into
a reusable logical render target. Static RGBA atlases are uploaded once;
per-frame work is bounded by an explicit sprite-command limit. The atlas
count and logical RGBA byte budget are independent. It shares the existing
letterbox and nearest-sampling policy with streaming frame presentation.
Exact software-renderer read-back covers draw order, transparency,
horizontal flipping, clipping, frame clearing and rejection without
changing the displayed pixels. Both rendering paths passed the explicitly
enabled native accelerated-window smoke in fresh validation run
1795582523569847945-3501164. No physical GPU timing, actual VRAM usage or
game-scene throughput is claimed.

An opt-in native diagnostic now uses the bounded persistent atlas path
with actual locally imported data. Exact logical RGBA readback matched
the CPU-packed atlas in two consecutive frames for each of two selections
(1,024 background tiles and 1,023 sprite tiles) on SDL OpenGL.
Readback is explicitly diagnostic and synchronises the renderer; it is
not part of the ordinary presentation loop. Physical GPU utilisation,
frame-time percentiles and peak resident/VRAM usage remain unmeasured.
